#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-6254" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$." :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151869 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/id #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "e-6255" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A$. Determine if $a_{1}, a_{2}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$.\nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=(-\\frac{1}{5})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gives us\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6272" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A$. Determine if $a_{1}, a_{2}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$. If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, that is to say,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gives us\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8feaa41f-b1f8-537e-91af-f44bf37fc57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149905} {:answer/id #uuid "92e03a33-1f22-53d9-b1ab-239a8479a2a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is not a basis of $Col(A)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149906} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c3362f-28f8-5ab3-bd47-688f7b472ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149907} {:answer/id #uuid "cb731d34-94e0-53f0-85b7-06babe8fdde6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149908} {:answer/id #uuid "63fa62a1-a0aa-50c5-abcb-b56f55365790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63fa62a1-a0aa-50c5-abcb-b56f55365790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149909} :answer-field/id #uuid "442908cd-f57d-5e58-a95a-bc9af22434b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149904}] :question/id #uuid "6cb6cc6e-d524-533e-a923-e69196a1f53b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34530" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, $a_{3}$ and $a_{4}$ be the respective columns of $A$.\nDetermine if $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is a basis of $Col(A)$.\nIf that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $a_{4} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
